<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory the Illuminator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 257–c. 331</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Gregory the Illuminator, Grigor Lusavorich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory the Illuminator was the founder and first head of the Armenian Church. According to tradition he was imprisoned for years in a pit at Khor Virap by King Tiridates III, then healed and converted the king, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as its state religion in the early 4th century, traditionally in 301. Consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at Caesarea, he organised the church, and his descendants held its leadership for generations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator.jpg" alt="Gregory the Illuminator" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.dallorto · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_the_Illuminator_mosaic_on_Pammakaristos_Church_in_Constantinople.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the Armenian tradition the standard life of Gregory derives from a fifth-century hagiographic history attributed to Agathangelos. It makes him the son of the Parthian nobleman Anak, who at the urging of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> assassinated the Arsacid king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Khosrov. The Armenian nobles put Anak and his family to death, but Gregory escaped with the help of his nurse and was taken to Caesarea in Cappadocia, where he received a Christian upbringing. Some modern scholars distrust this account. Marie Louise Chaumont suggested he was probably a Greek from Cappadocia. According to Movses Khorenatsi, Gregory married Mariam and had two sons, Aristaces and Vrtanes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gregory entered the service of King Tiridates III, son of the murdered Khosrov. When he refused to sacrifice to the goddess Anahit, the king had him tortured, and on learning whose son he was, had him thrown into the deep pit of Khor Virap near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, where he stayed for thirteen or fifteen years. Agathangelos says he was brought out after the king's sister Khosrovidukht had a vision. He then healed the king, whom the history describes as driven into animal madness for his sins, and Tiridates and his court accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>Gregory raised shrines to the martyrs Gayane and Hripsime at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>, later called Ejmiatsin, and destroyed pagan temples. He was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> at Caesarea by Leontius of Caesarea, and his successors went there to be confirmed until the death of Nerses I. He built churches in place of the temples, founded schools teaching in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, and baptized the king, the army and the people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. The conversion is traditionally dated to 301, but modern scholarship considers a date near 314 more likely, and holds that the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was more gradual than Agathangelos depicts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gregory appointed his younger son Aristaces as his successor and lived as an ascetic in the cave of Manē in Upper Armenia. According to Khorenatsi he was last seen after Aristaces returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, and he died in seclusion, buried by shepherds who did not know who he was. His descendants, the Gregorids, held the patriarchate of Armenia, with some interruptions, until the death of Patriarch Isaac in the fifth century. The Armenian Church is sometimes called lusavorchakan, &quot;of the Illuminator&quot;, or Gregorian. The Eastern Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches commemorate him on 30 September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gregory the Illuminator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity reached the country from Armenia, and according to tradition King Urnayr was baptised in the fourth century in connection with the mission of <b>Gregory the Illuminator</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tiridates III adopted Christianity around 301 through <b>Gregory the Illuminator</b>, and Etchmiadzin cathedral was founded.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
